    {
      "id": "a1-u02-d04-t01",
      "before": "a1-u02-d04-e01",
      "title": { "en": "Czy and nie" },
      "body": { "en": "Put czy at the start to make a yes-or-no question: czy jesteś z Polski? In speech it is often left out, and then only the voice rises. Nie before a verb means \"not\": nie jestem z Polski. So nie, nie jestem is \"no, I am not\"." }
    },
    {
      "id": "a1-u02-d04-t02",
      "before": "a1-u02-d04-e04",
      "title": { "en": "Pan and pani" },
      "body": { "en": "To an adult you do not know well, say pan (to a man) or pani (to a woman) instead of ty. The verb then takes the form used with on and ona: czy pan jest z Polski? Jak się pan ma? is the polite jak się masz?" }
    }
  ],
